markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Module Overview

This professional learning module is made up of **five lessons:**

<div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 20px; max-width: 900px; margin: auto;">

  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/d7a2764589820d4023ca876e49c892c1-image-1740592077414.png" alt="Lesson 1 Icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
            <h3>Lesson 1 - Introduction to Web Apps</h3>
            <div><strong>⭐️ You are here!⭐️</strong></div>
            <div>This lesson introduces what a web app is, how users interact with one, and the core parts of a website. You will build a shared vocabulary for the structural components you will be teaching throughout the unit.<br><br><em>10 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>

  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/3d11b3154389d2caa405683ffda44ff6-image-1740592165845.png" alt="Lesson 2 Icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
            <h3>Lesson 2 - Intro to CSS</h3>
            <div>This lesson covers CSS and how it shapes the user experience of a web app. You will learn the POUR accessibility principles and how to apply them when styling a page.<br><br><em>20 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>

  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/76c4e5b2632895e2ff6513ffb63ee5fc-image-1785425093199.png" alt="Lesson 3 Icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
            <h3>Lesson 3 - Interactivity in Web Apps</h3>
            <div>This lesson introduces JavaScript as the language that makes a web app respond to users. You will learn how event listeners and handlers work together with the DOM to update a page in real time.<br><br><em>25 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>

  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/3f12c0d8b05f89f5ee40071c08233249-image-1785425200781.png" alt="Lesson 4 Icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #E4E2F8; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
            <h3>Lesson 4 - JS Functions, Conditionals, and Loops</h3>
            <div>This lesson covers three core building blocks of JavaScript: reusable functions, if/else conditionals, and loops. You will see how these pieces let students build programs that make decisions and repeat actions.<br><br><em>25 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>

  <div style="display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 15px;">
        <img src="https://images.code.org/b39dda06cfbafec7aef8b85d0c3caed1-image-1740592349350.png" alt="Lesson 5 Icon" style="width: 20%; max-width: 120px; height: auto;">
        <div style="flex: 1; background: #f7f8fa; padding: 15px; border-radius: 10px; font-size: 14pt;">
            <h3>Lesson 5 - Preparing for the Final Project</h3>
            <div>This lesson focuses on key instructional strategies for guiding students through their final project, including collaboration methods, structured feedback, and time management planning.<br><br><em>10 minutes</em></div>
        </div>
    </div>

</div>




---

## Engaging with This Module

Feel free to complete as much or as little of this module as is useful for you and your students! 

Stop, start, and revisit content as you see fit. We hope you'll find these activities useful to start teaching this unit. 

You'll be able to give Code.org your feedback on the last level of this module.

![]()
MARKDOWN
